# Dagric OS update channel.
#
# This is how a machine that is ALREADY INSTALLED receives fixes to Dagric
# itself. Debian's own security updates arrive through unattended-upgrades and
# never needed this; what needed it is everything in /usr/bin/dagric-*,
# /usr/share/dagric and the desktop defaults — which, until this file was
# enabled, could only ever be changed by reinstalling the operating system.
#
# The host is the download site rather than a dedicated repo.dagric.com. It is
# already on HTTPS, already deployed by the same command that publishes the
# download page, and is a name the project controls — so the channel needs no
# new infrastructure and cannot be lost with a lapsed domain.
#
# Signed by the same key that signs the ISO checksums (6CE37402BA0A0EF8), so an
# owner who verified their download is already set up to trust their updates.
#
# THIS FILE IS ONLY HALF OF IT, AND THE OTHER HALF USED TO BE MISSING.
# A source line delivers nothing unless dpkg has a record of the packages it
# serves, and for one release it had none: the four dagric-* packages were
# assembled straight into site/repo while their contents reached the ISO through
# includes.chroot, so `apt upgrade` on a sold machine could never offer a fix to
# the wizard, the manual, the wallpapers or the security baseline. build.sh now
# builds the same .debs from the tree it is about to ship and hands them to
# live-build as local packages, which is what registers them. If you ever remove
# that step, this file goes back to being decoration.
#
# WHAT ARRIVES SILENTLY AND WHAT DOES NOT. Debian's own security updates land on
# their own through unattended-upgrades. Of Dagric's four packages, exactly ONE
# does the same, and the other three wait to be asked.
#
#   dagric-security-policy   INSTALLS ITSELF. It carries the kernel hardening
#                            sysctls and the APT policy — the security baseline,
#                            and nothing an owner looks at. Its own description
#                            says it is "versioned so the baseline can evolve via
#                            updates", and that was untrue for as long as nothing
#                            in the origins matched it: a hardening regression
#                            fixed through this channel sat in Discover until
#                            somebody happened to look. Silence is defensible for
#                            exactly this class of update and no other.
#
#   dagric-tools             WAIT TO BE ASKED. These change what the machine
#   dagric-branding          LOOKS like and how it BEHAVES — the wizard, the Hub,
#   dagric-desktop-defaults  the manual, the wallpapers, the desktop defaults.
#                            Replacing the owner's own tools behind their back is
#                            a different promise from "security fixes land
#                            without you having to think about it", and not one
#                            this product makes. They appear in Discover as
#                            updates the owner clicks, like any other app.
#
# The mechanism is in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52dagric-unattended, NOT in a build
# hook: Origins-Pattern admits origin=Dagric, and a Package-Blacklist holds those
# three back by name. That file is a conffile of dagric-security-policy, so this
# split can itself be corrected by an update — which is the whole reason it
# stopped being written by 0300-hardening.hook.chroot.
#
# This paragraph used to say that nothing from Origin: Dagric ever installs
# itself. That stopped being true the day the baseline was allowed through, and
# it is the sentence an owner reads to learn what this channel does — so if the
# policy changes again, change it here in the same commit.
deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/dagric.gpg] https://dagric-os.web.app/repo trixie main
